# Docker build context for the server image.
#
# THE ONE TRAP THIS FILE EXISTS TO AVOID: excluding a workspace manifest. The
# Dockerfile runs `npm ci` against the root lockfile, and npm resolves the
# WHOLE workspace tree in one pass - so a missing `client/package.json` fails
# the install outright, even though nothing in the server image imports the
# client. All three of `shared/`, `server/` and `client/package.json` must
# reach the context.
#
# ALLOWLIST: `*` drops everything and each `!` adds one thing back, so the
# context is what the server needs and cannot silently grow. The re-includes
# are TOP-LEVEL names, then pruned from the inside - re-including a nested
# path under a directory `*` already excluded depends on how the daemon prunes
# excluded directories, which is not worth relying on for a manifest the build
# cannot proceed without.

*

# --- install inputs -------------------------------------------------------
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!.npmrc

# --- the two workspaces the image compiles --------------------------------
!shared
!server

# --- the client, for its manifest ALONE -----------------------------------
# Needed so `npm ci` can resolve the workspace tree. Its sources are not:
# the Vite bundle is built by the deploy workflow and uploaded to Bloxity
# frontend hosting, never served from this container.
!client
client/src
client/dist
client/index.html
client/vite.config.ts
client/tsconfig.json

# --- re-exclude build litter inside the paths added back above ------------
# A stale local `dist` would otherwise be copied over the freshly compiled
# output, and `node_modules` would defeat `npm ci` and could carry binaries
# built for the wrong OS.
node_modules
**/node_modules
shared/dist
server/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.log

# --- never ship the runtime save file -------------------------------------
# server/data holds real player profiles. It is gitignored, and baking a copy
# into an image would ship one player's progression to everybody who pulls it.
data
server/data
